@title  PCSE00120 - New Game: title screen, difficulty, both movies, the Velvet Room, name entry, the tarot reading, the intro cutscene, arrival in Inaba, FIRST PLAYER CONTROL
@game   PCSE00120
@shot-every 1000

# WHAT THIS RECIPE PROVES
# ----------------------
# The title boots, reaches its title screen, starts a NEW GAME at NORMAL difficulty, plays
# its two front-end movies, reaches the Velvet Room, ENTERS A NAME, sits through Igor's
# tarot reading, goes on through the opening cutscene movie and the arrival in Inaba, and
# ends in the FIELD under player control, walking the protagonist down the street. Every screen along the way
# renders through the recompiler (no fixed-function fallback), the title music and confirm
# sounds play, and both movies decode with their sound.
#
# THE ONE THING THAT COSTS A SESSION IF IT IS NOT WRITTEN DOWN: this title sits on a BLACK
# screen forever with no input, looking perfectly healthy while it does.
#
# THIS FILE IS ON THE PACED TIMELINE. The display queue is bounded now, so the title runs at
# its OWN 30 flips a second (its display callback waits TWO vblanks) instead of the 60 an
# unbounded queue let it reach. Every frame number below belongs to that rate; a recipe
# authored against the old one plays its inputs at roughly twice the speed the screens
# arrive. The lead-in through the notices was re-timed through game time (a clock-mapping
# script over the old recipe); everything from the name entry on was authored
# by driving a resident `session` and MEASURING where each screen lands, because a clock
# mapping cannot know that the movies now play at their true rate and so take longer.
#
# WHERE THE SCREENS ARE (measured, GPU shots from the session that authored the tail):
#   f0995  the boot notices (Vox Populi / PSN prompt) - a cross dismisses each
#   f1197  the P4G logo splash, then the CRIWARE splash
#   f1703  the yellow title screen
#   f1900  the main menu: NEW GAME / CONFIG / LOAD GAME / TV LISTINGS, NEW GAME selected
#   f2815  the difficulty select (NORMAL preselected); a cross takes it, then Yes
#   f3776  the first movie (the truck in the fog)
#   f5040  the opening anime movie - plays into the Velvet Room
#   f7063  the Velvet Room: Igor's "guest with an intriguing destiny" dialogue
#   f7900  name entry, LAST name, with one letter already typed
#   f8194  name entry, FIRST name
#   f8262  "> Proceed?", then Yes
#   f8458  back in the Velvet Room; the tarot reading starts
#   f10234 the reading ("The Moon, in the upright position")
#   f12604 the intro cutscene movie (the beach), f13000 the news desk, f13504 the train,
#          f15904 the missing-persons broadcast
#   f17704 Dojima at Inaba station; crosses every 44 frames through the car and gas station
#   f23600 FIELD MODE, "Shopping District, South" - the stick walks the protagonist (GAMEPLAY)
#
# HOW THE NAME ENTRY WORKS, because two probes read it as broken and it is not:
#   - a DIRECTION press held 4 frames moves the keyboard cursor ONE cell. Held 8 it moves
#     TWO: the title's input manager computes its own edges and auto-repeats at 7 frames
#     delay / 3 frames interval, faithfully.
#   - on the LAST-name screen `START End` is GREYED OUT. The field is left by FILLING its
#     eight characters (or with R); the screen then advances to the first name on its own.
#     `< L` and `R >` move the caret within the field, they do not switch fields.
#   - on the FIRST-name screen START is lit and ENDS the name: it raises `> Proceed?`, a
#     cross opens Yes/No, and a second cross takes Yes.
#   A phone report of "the START button is greyed out" is the last-name screen, working.
#
# There is deliberately NO `@sig` and no `@assert`. No live-state address is known for this
# title, and this run decodes two movies through the HOST's video and AAC decoders, so its
# frames are not a cross-machine invariant. [[vitaslop-a-recipe-through-a-movie-is-not-a-render-oracle]]

